---
// The anxious attachment test.
//
// Deliberately NOT under /tools/ — that path is noindex,nofollow and
// excluded from the sitemap, because those pages are the private
// workspace used to build TikTok slideshows. This one is the opposite: a
// public page that is meant to rank, so it uses BaseLayout and sits at
// /quiz/ where later tests can join it.
//
// Everything runs in the browser. No answer is sent anywhere, which is
// both true and the same promise the app makes, so the page says so.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { QUESTIONS, BANDS, MAX_SCORE } from '../../data/anxious-attachment-test';

const title = 'Anxious Attachment Test: Free 2-Minute Quiz';
const description =
  'A free anxious attachment test. 12 questions on how you handle distance, silence and reassurance, with what your result means and what helps.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is this anxious attachment test accurate?',
    a: 'It is a structured way to notice a pattern, not a clinical measure. It is not a validated instrument and it cannot diagnose anything. What it is good for is putting words to something you may already half-know, and it is most useful when you answer about how you usually are rather than how you were last week.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What counts as a high anxious attachment score?',
    a: 'Scores run from 0 to 36. Under 9 suggests distance rarely registers as threat. Between 9 and 17 is a pattern that shows up under stress. Between 18 and 26 is a consistent one. Above 27 means these reactions are showing up strongly and across relationships.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can anxious attachment change?',
    a: 'Yes. Attachment patterns are learned expectations rather than fixed traits, and they shift through repeated experience of closeness that does not disappear the moment there is distance. That can come from a steady relationship, from therapy, or from deliberately practising a different response. It is slow, and it is real.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is anxious attachment the same as being clingy?',
    a: 'No. Clinginess is one behaviour it can produce. The pattern underneath is that uncertainty registers as danger faster than it does for other people, so the behaviour is an attempt to make the uncertainty stop. Two people with the same underlying anxiety can look completely different from the outside.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do my answers get saved or sent anywhere?',
    a: 'No. The whole test runs in your browser. Nothing is uploaded, nothing is stored, and closing the tab ends it. That is the same principle the Chakam app works on, where your chat is analysed on your device.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What should I do if I score high?',
    a: 'Take it as information rather than a verdict. Reading about the pattern helps, naming the feeling before acting on it helps more, and talking to a therapist who works with attachment helps most. If it is affecting your sleep or how you feel about yourself day to day, that is worth taking to a professional rather than a quiz.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL('/', Astro.site).href },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Quizzes', item: new URL('/quiz/', Astro.site).href },
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 3,
      name: 'Anxious Attachment Test',
      item: new URL('/quiz/anxious-attachment-test/', Astro.site).href,
    },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} — Chakam`} description={description} active="quiz">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700;800;900&display=swap"
    />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="quiz-wrap">
    <header class="quiz-head">
      <a class="quiz-backlink" href="/quiz/">&larr; All quizzes</a>
      <span class="quiz-kicker">Free test</span>
      <h1>Anxious Attachment Test</h1>
      <p class="quiz-sub">
        Twelve questions about how you handle silence, distance and reassurance.
        Takes about two minutes. Nothing you answer leaves your browser.
      </p>
      <p class="quiz-note">
        This is not a diagnosis and not a clinical instrument. It is a structured
        way to see a pattern you may already suspect.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- The test. One question at a time: a 12-question wall gets abandoned. -->
    <section class="quiz-card" id="quizCard" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="quiz-progress" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="quiz-progress-fill" id="progressFill"></div>
      </div>
      <p class="quiz-counter"><span id="qNum">1</span> of {QUESTIONS.length}</p>
      <h2 class="quiz-question" id="questionText">{QUESTIONS[0].text}</h2>
      <div class="quiz-options" id="options"></div>
      <button type="button" class="quiz-back" id="backBtn" hidden>&larr; Back</button>
    </section>

    <!-- Result. Hidden until the last answer lands. -->
    <section class="quiz-result" id="result" hidden>
      <div class="result-band">
        <span class="result-kicker">Your result</span>
        <h2 id="bandLabel"></h2>
        <div class="result-meter" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="result-meter-fill" id="meterFill"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="result-score"><span id="scoreNum">0</span> out of {MAX_SCORE}</p>
        <p class="result-summary" id="bandSummary"></p>
      </div>

      <div class="result-detail" id="bandDetail"></div>

      <div class="result-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn primary" id="downloadBtn">Download your result</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="retakeBtn">Take it again</button>
      </div>

      <div class="result-reads">
        <h3>Read next</h3>
        <ul id="bandReads"></ul>
      </div>

      <div class="result-app">
        <h3>That is what you say about yourself</h3>
        <p>
          Every test like this one is self-report. It asks how you think you behave
          and trusts the answer. Chakam does the other half: import a real
          conversation and it shows what actually happened in it, including who
          reaches out first, how reply times have drifted, and whether the distance
          you are feeling shows up in the messages.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app also carries the other twelve tests, none of which are on this
          site:
        </p>
        <ul class="app-quiz-list">
          <li>What&rsquo;s Your Attachment Style? <span>all four patterns, not just this one</span></li>
          <li>What&rsquo;s Your Texting Persona?</li>
          <li>Red Flag Radar</li>
          <li>How Do You Handle Conflict?</li>
          <li>How Do You Make Up After a Fight?</li>
          <li>Who Carries the Relationship?</li>
          <li>What&rsquo;s Your Love Language?</li>
          <li>What&rsquo;s Your Attraction Style?</li>
          <li>Push-Pull or Real Chemistry?</li>
          <li>How Do You Handle Endings?</li>
          <li>When You&rsquo;re Called Out, What Do You Actually Do?</li>
          <li>Do You Show Narcissistic Traits?</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="store-row">
          <a class="store-badge" href="https://apps.apple.com/ng/app/chakam-chat-wrapped-red-flag/id6792742396">
            <img src="/assets/img/apple_icon.png" alt="App Store" />
            <span class="store-text">
              <span class="store-eyebrow">Download on the</span>
              <span class="store-name">App Store</span>
            </span>
          </a>
          <a class="store-badge" href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.chakam.chatanalyzer">
            <img src="/assets/img/png-clipart-goggle-playstore-icon-google-play-computer-icons-android-play-button-angle-rectangle.png" alt="Google Play" />
            <span class="store-text">
              <span class="store-eyebrow">Get it on</span>
              <span class="store-name">Google Play</span>
            </span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The shareable card, rendered off-screen and captured on demand.
         Fixed 1080x1350 so the export is the same on every device. -->
    <div class="card-stage" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="share-card" id="shareCard">
        <div class="share-inner">
          <span class="share-wordmark">chakam</span>
          <span class="share-kicker">Anxious attachment test</span>
          <h2 class="share-band" id="shareBand"></h2>
          <p class="share-line" id="shareLine"></p>
          <div class="share-meter"><div class="share-meter-fill" id="shareMeter"></div></div>
          <p class="share-score" id="shareScore"></p>
          <span class="share-cta">chakam.site/quiz</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <article class="quiz-prose">
      <h2>What Is Anxious Attachment?</h2>
      <p>
        Anxious attachment describes a pattern where closeness matters a great
        deal and distance registers as threat. It is not the same as being
        clingy, needy or dramatic. The behaviours people notice from the outside,
        the double texts and the checking and the reassurance-seeking, are the
        visible end of something simpler: uncertainty feels dangerous, and those
        actions are an attempt to make it stop.
      </p>
      <p>
        It usually forms early, in a context where needing someone did not
        reliably work out. A child whose bids for comfort were sometimes met and
        sometimes not learns that connection is unpredictable and has to be
        monitored. That is a sensible adaptation to a real environment, and it
        travels into adult relationships as a heightened sensitivity to distance.
      </p>

      <h2>How This Anxious Attachment Test Works</h2>
      <p>
        The twelve questions all point at one thing: what happens in you when
        there is a gap. A slow reply, a weekend apart, an unresolved
        disagreement, a message left on read. Each answer scores from 0 to 3, so
        results run from 0 to {MAX_SCORE}, and the bands describe how much of
        your attention this pattern is taking rather than putting you in a box.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things make the result more useful. Answer about how you usually are
        rather than how you have been this week, since a single difficult stretch
        pulls the same reactions out of almost anyone. And answer about the
        relationships you have actually had, not the person you would like to be.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Test Cannot Do</h2>
      <p>
        It cannot diagnose you. It is not a validated psychological instrument,
        it was not developed through clinical trials, and a score here is not a
        finding about your mental health. Attachment researchers use measures
        built and tested over decades for exactly that reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it can do is give shape to something vague. Most people arriving at
        a page like this already suspect the answer and want to know whether the
        thing they keep doing has a name. It does, it is common, and it can
        change.
      </p>

      <h2>Can Anxious Attachment Be Changed?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, and the mechanism is unglamorous. Attachment patterns are learned
        expectations about what closeness will cost you, and expectations update
        through repeated evidence. Closeness that does not turn into control.
        Distance that does not turn into abandonment. Conflict that ends in
        repair rather than in someone leaving.
      </p>
      <p>
        That evidence can come from a steady relationship, from therapy, or from
        deliberately practising a different response to the same trigger. What
        does not work is trying to need less. The aim is not to stop wanting
        reassurance, it is to stop needing it urgently enough that it drives what
        you do next.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <div class="quiz-faq">
          <h3>{f.q}</h3>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </article>
  </div>

  <!-- is:global on purpose. The answer buttons are created in JS, so they
       never receive Astro's generated scope attribute and scoped rules
       would not match them. Every selector below is namespaced (.quiz-,
       .result-, .share-, .card-stage) so nothing leaks into other pages. -->
  <style is:global>
        /* ---- header ---- */
                                /* ---- the test ---- */
    .quiz-card {
